<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import QuickLog from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/QuickLog.abide'
import quickLogSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/QuickLog.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import lastActivitySource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/LastActivity.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import activitySource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/activity.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import quickLogTestSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/tests/QuickLog.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import RecentActivity from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/RecentActivity.abide'
import recentActivitySource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/RecentActivity.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import recentActivityTestSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/tests/RecentActivity.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import AccountFeed from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/AccountFeed.abide'
import accountFeedSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/AccountFeed.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import feedSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/feed.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import logToFeedSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/logToFeed.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import feedsSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/feeds.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import accountFeedTestSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/tests/AccountFeed.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import ActivityHistory from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/ActivityHistory.abide'
import activityHistorySource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/ActivityHistory.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import olderActivitySource from 'crm-05/src/activity/olderActivity.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import activityHistoryTestSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/tests/ActivityHistory.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import CsvExport from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/CsvExport.abide'
import csvExportSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/CsvExport.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import exportCsvSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/exportCsv.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import csvExportTestSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/tests/CsvExport.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import Wallboard from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/Wallboard.abide'
import wallboardSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/lib/Wallboard.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import tickerSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/ticker.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import wallboardTestSource from 'crm-05/src/activity/tests/Wallboard.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>A channel is a <code>reactive</code> holding an array. Anything that can reach it appends
    with <code>patch</code>, and anything that can reach it reads. Put one behind a
    <code>GET</code> and every open tab follows it. A handler that yields sends each value as it
    is produced, from the same address that answers with the whole.</p>

    <Section title="A channel is an array anything can append to">
        <Example
            id="crm-05-channel"
            app="crm-05"
            title="`reactive<Activity[]>([])` — a channel anything can reach"
            summary="A quick-log bar on a contact, and a line above it that reports the last touch. Log a call and watch a component the bar never names."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/activity.ts': activitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/QuickLog.abide': quickLogSource,
                'src/activity/lib/LastActivity.abide': lastActivitySource,
                'src/activity/tests/QuickLog.test.ts': quickLogTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <QuickLog />
        </Example>

        <p><code>activity.ts</code> holds the channel and the one function that writes it. The bar
        imports the function, the readout imports the channel, and neither imports the other. No
        prop is threaded between them and no provider wraps them.</p>

        <p>The readout derives <code>last</code> as <code>activity.at(-1)</code>, and follows the
        channel the way any derived value follows what it read. Opened with an array, the channel
        has landed: it is <code>ready()</code> at once and never <code>busy()</code> where it is
        written.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A feed keeps its last few entries">
        <Example
            id="crm-05-recent"
            app="crm-05"
            title="`patch` with a trim — the last three entries"
            summary="The same channel listed in full, already three entries long. Log a meeting and watch the top of the list."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/RecentActivity.abide': recentActivitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/activity.ts': activitySource,
                'src/activity/tests/RecentActivity.test.ts': recentActivityTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <RecentActivity />
        </Example>

        <p>The trim is one line in the patch that appends: <code>if (entries.length &gt; 3)
        entries.shift()</code>. <code>patch</code> hands you the array the channel holds and
        changes it in place, so the push and the trim are one write. abide puts no cap on the
        array. The condition is yours: a count here, an age or a byte total where that is what
        bounds it. Keep the push and the trim in one function every writer calls, and the cap
        belongs to the channel rather than to whichever caller remembered it.</p>

        <p>A value keeps no past of its own. Where you want what came before, make the value the
        array and append where you write, as here. An undo stack is the same: an array the app
        pushes into.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A feed on the server reaches every open tab">
        <Example
            id="crm-05-live-feed"
            app="crm-05"
            title="`GET` + `POST` — one account's feed, every tab"
            summary="The activity feed on Hopper & Co. Open this page in a second tab, log a call in either, and both lists grow."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/AccountFeed.abide': accountFeedSource,
                'src/activity/lib/feeds.ts': feedsSource,
                'src/activity/feed.ts': feedSource,
                'src/activity/logToFeed.ts': logToFeedSource,
                'src/activity/tests/AccountFeed.test.ts': accountFeedTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <AccountFeed />
        </Example>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'reactive(fn, { global: true })'}</code></td><td>the feeds, one per account, shared by every caller</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET(feeds)</code></td><td>its address: a tab reading it follows it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST(…)</code></td><td>the way in: a handler that patches a feed</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>global: true</code> puts the feeds in one cache for the whole process. Leave it
        out and a value on the server belongs to one request, so every caller gets a feed of their
        own and nobody sees anybody's calls. The body takes <code>account</code>, so there is one
        feed per account, and <code>ttl</code> lets one nobody has written to for an hour go.</p>

        <p>A tab reading <code>feed</code> is subscribed to it, over one web socket that every
        followed <code>GET</code> on the page shares. The button does not paint its own entry. It
        calls <code>logToFeed</code>, the handler patches the feed, and what the patch wrote goes
        to every tab following it, this one included. Each write travels as a
        <code>PatchOp</code>, so a new entry is one <code>add</code> rather than the whole
        feed.</p>

        <p>The server writes each entry: its id, its kind and its time. A caller can only pick a
        kind the server knows, so nobody forges an entry, and who may log at all is the
        <code>POST</code>'s own middleware. The call hands back the same <code>Reactive</code>
        the handler had, its <code>Produced</code> type included, so <code>entries.length</code>
        and <code>{'{#for entry of entries}'}</code> read it like any array.</p>

        <p>The feed lives in memory. A restart starts it empty, and each instance of the app holds
        its own. Activity anyone must scroll back past that belongs in your database, behind an
        ordinary handler.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A handler that yields sends each entry as it is read">
        <Example
            id="crm-05-stream"
            app="crm-05"
            title="`async function*` — the archive, an entry at a time"
            summary="Hopper & Co's older activity, read from a slow archive. The list fills an entry at a time."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/ActivityHistory.abide': activityHistorySource,
                'src/activity/olderActivity.ts': olderActivitySource,
                'src/activity/tests/ActivityHistory.test.ts': activityHistoryTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ActivityHistory />
        </Example>

        <p>A handler written as an async generator streams. Each <code>yield</code> goes out as it
        happens, one JSON value per line, and a proxy in front passes each line on rather than
        holding it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>You get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#for entry of entries}'}</code></td><td>every entry so far, and each one after as it lands</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>entries.busy()</code></td><td>true until the stream closes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await entries</code></td><td>every entry, at the close</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'for await (const entry of entries)'}</code></td><td>each entry of the run, once, ending at the close</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>for await (… of s)</code> walks the run through
        <code>s[Symbol.asyncIterator]</code>: from its first entry to its close, so a reader that
        starts halfway still gets every entry. On a value that does not stream it yields the
        settled value once and ends. The test walks the run.</p>

        <p>A page rendered on the server is not held for an unfinished stream. What the stream had
        produced is inline, and the browser picks up the rest. The transcript held for that
        handoff is capped by <code>ABIDE_MAX_STREAM_BUFFER_SIZE</code>, in bytes, unbounded by
        default. Past it the browser runs the handler again.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="An export is written as its rows are read">
        <Example
            id="crm-05-csv"
            app="crm-05"
            title="`bytes(values)` — a CSV written as the cursor reaches each row"
            summary="An account's activity as a CSV download. Each line goes into the file as its row is read."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/CsvExport.abide': csvExportSource,
                'src/activity/exportCsv.ts': exportCsvSource,
                'src/activity/tests/CsvExport.test.ts': csvExportTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CsvExport />
        </Example>

        <p>A helper fixes how a stream is framed, and nothing a caller asks for moves it off.
        Each takes <code>Values&lt;T&gt;</code>: an array, an iterable, an async iterable or a
        <code>ReadableStream</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The handler returns</th><th>The caller gets</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>an async generator</td><td>JSON Lines, or events for a caller asking for <code>text/event-stream</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>jsonl(values)</code></td><td>JSON Lines, whatever the caller asks for: a list sent a line at a time</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sse(values)</code></td><td>server-sent events, whatever the caller asks for</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bytes(values)</code></td><td>the chunks as they are, <code>application/octet-stream</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A stream whose first chunk is binary is sent as bytes without the helper.
        <code>bytes</code> says so before there is a first chunk, so an account with no activity
        downloads an empty file rather than an empty JSON answer.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A wallboard is sent events whatever it asks for">
        <Example
            id="crm-05-ticker"
            app="crm-05"
            title="`sse(values)` — events, whoever asks"
            summary="A wallboard by the sales floor reads today's deal moves. It sends no Accept header, and what comes back is events anyway."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/Wallboard.abide': wallboardSource,
                'src/activity/ticker.ts': tickerSource,
                'src/activity/tests/Wallboard.test.ts': wallboardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Wallboard />
        </Example>

        <p>A wallboard script, like <code>curl</code>, asks for nothing in particular, and an
        async generator answers that caller with JSON Lines. <code>sse</code> fixes the answer as
        events, <code>data: </code> and a JSON value per event, so the board reads it with the
        event-stream parser it already has. An <code>EventSource</code> asks for events by name,
        and gets them from any streaming handler without the helper.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Watch a feed from the terminal">
        <Code code={`abide watch activity/feed account=hopper-co --url http://localhost:3000`} />

        <p><code>abide watch &lt;address&gt; [args]</code> follows a <code>GET</code> and prints
        its value, then the value again after each patch. The address is the handler's route
        without its leading <code>/</code>, the args are <code>name=value</code> words, and
        <code>--url</code> names the running app. Inside the <code>abide</code> shell it is
        <code>watch activity/feed account=hopper-co</code>.</p>

        <p><code>watch</code> asks the <code>GET</code> for the same event stream any caller may ask for,
        so what you see is what each tab is sent. With <code>curl</code> it is the value, then
        each patch as <code>PatchOp</code>s:</p>

        <Code code={`curl -N -H 'Accept: text/event-stream' \\
  'http://localhost:3000/activity/feed?account=hopper-co'

event: value
id: 1792159056295002
data: [{"id":"9dcf…","kind":"Call","at":"13:57:43"}]

event: patch
id: 1792159056295003
data: [{"op":"add","path":"/-","value":{"id":"344f…","kind":"Email",…}}]`} />

        <p><code>watch</code> only reads. To log from a terminal, call
        <code>logToFeed</code> as a tab does, and its middleware decides whether the shell may.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/guide/add-pages')}>Add pages</a> — a page per company, the chrome
            they share, and what a reader sees when one fails</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
